---
import BlogPost from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What does RSVP stand for in speed reading?',
    a: 'RSVP stands for Rapid Serial Visual Presentation. It displays text one word or short phrase at a time at a fixed focal point on the screen so your eyes do not have to scan across lines.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the Optimal Recognition Point (ORP) in RSVP?',
    a: 'The Optimal Recognition Point is the letter slightly left of a word’s center where the eye naturally focuses. Highlighting and anchoring that letter in one spot reduces micro-adjustments between words.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does RSVP speed reading hurt comprehension?',
    a: 'At moderate speeds (around 300 to 400 WPM) on familiar text, RSVP works well. At extreme speeds or on dense technical prose, comprehension drops because you cannot glance back, which is why BriskRead lets you switch to Focus, Flow, or Page mode anytime.',
  },
];
---

<BlogPost slug="rsvp-reading-explained" faqs={faqs} related={['best-online-speed-readers', 'audio-sync-speed-reading', 'how-to-speed-read-online']}>
  <p class="lede">
    RSVP means <strong>Rapid Serial Visual Presentation</strong>: words appear one after another at a fixed place on the screen so your eyes stop hunting along a line. It is the core of many online flash readers, including Flash mode in <a href="/rsvp-reader">BriskRead’s RSVP reader</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How RSVP works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Text is split into words (or short phrases)</li>
    <li>Each unit is shown for a duration based on your WPM</li>
    <li>A focal letter (often the Optimal Recognition Point) is emphasized</li>
    <li>Punctuation can add a brief pause so meaning can land</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why people use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Less horizontal eye movement (saccades)</li>
    <li>Harder to re-read the same line by habit</li>
    <li>Clearer sense of pace than free scrolling</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits of RSVP</h2>
  <p>
    RSVP is not magic. On unfamiliar, formula-heavy, or legal text, forced flash speed can destroy comprehension. That is why serious tools also offer <a href="/how-it-works">Focus, Flow, Bionic, and Page modes</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The research agrees. A 2016 review of reading science found a firm trade-off between speed and accuracy, and noted that RSVP removes the quick glances back that readers use to repair understanding (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/1529100615623267" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rayner et al., 2016</a>). A 2017 study of speed-reading apps found they did not improve comprehension (<a href="https://doi.org/10.5406/amerjpsyc.130.2.0183" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Acklin &amp; Papesh, 2017</a>). If single words feel too sparse, <a href="/bionic-reading">bionic reading</a> keeps the whole page in view.
  </p>

  <h2>RSVP + audio</h2>
  <p>
    Pairing RSVP with speech (Audio Sync) is dual-channel presentation: vision and hearing track the same token stream. See <a href="/blog/audio-sync-speed-reading">why that matters</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Demo RSVP free on the <a href="/">BriskRead homepage</a>: start around 350 WPM and lower the slider until words stay meaningful, or check your baseline first with the <a href="/reading-speed-test">reading speed test</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BlogPost>
